  {
    "number": 4,
    "title": "Proteins and Lipids: Do They Cause the Glucose Spike?",
    "duration": "80 minutes (2 x 40-minute periods)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 1.2: Chemicals of Life",
    "aresKeywords": "protein, amino acids, biuret test, lipid, fatty acids, glycerol, grease-spot test, emulsion test, organic compounds, blood glucose",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "Learners investigate proteins and lipids in beans, groundnuts, milk, nyama choma and mandazi, and compare them with carbohydrates to decide whether they cause the glucose spike in Wanjiru's graph.",
      "knowledge": "Proteins are organic compounds made of amino acids and are detected by the biuret test (blue to violet/purple). Lipids are organic compounds made of fatty acids and glycerol and are detected by the grease-spot test (translucent spot) and the emulsion test (cloudy white layer). Carbohydrates (Lesson 3) are made of glucose units and are tested with iodine and Benedict's. Wanjiru's meal has about 20 g protein and about 10 g fat, compared with about 70 g carbohydrate in ugali alone, and the meal data link carbohydrates, not proteins or lipids, to the glucose rise from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L.",
      "skills": "Carrying out the biuret, grease-spot and emulsion tests safely; recording results in a table; comparing results with evidence from Lesson 3; using evidence to argue which food chemicals cause the glucose spike (NGSS SEP: Planning and Carrying Out Investigations; Analyzing and Interpreting Data; Engaging in Argument from Evidence).",
      "attitudes": "Curiosity about what is in everyday foods; responsibility and safety when handling chemicals; respect for peers' ideas during discussion; appreciation of the roles of proteins and lipids in the body.",
      "keyInquiry": "1. What are the chemicals of life? 2. How do the chemicals of life contribute to the functioning of living organisms?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lesson 2 classified carbohydrates, proteins and lipids as organic compounds, and Lesson 3 linked ugali starch to the glucose spike using iodine and Benedict's tests. Lesson 4 completes the main food chemical classes by testing proteins and lipids, showing they are chemically different and do not cause the spike, which sets up Lesson 5 (enzymes) and Lesson 6 (absorption, respiration, insulin).",
      "safetyNotes": "Wear safety goggles and aprons. Biuret reagent (or dilute sodium hydroxide with dilute copper sulphate) is irritant: wear gloves, avoid skin and eye contact, and rinse spills with plenty of water. Ethanol for the emulsion test is flammable: keep away from flames and use only small amounts (2 cm3). Never taste any test material in the laboratory. Check for groundnut or milk allergies before the lesson and let affected learners handle only sealed samples or act as recorders. Wash hands after the practical and dispose of waste as directed by Mr Otieno."
    },
    "overview": "Mr Otieno's class returns to Wanjiru's graph, which showed blood glucose rising from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L at 60 minutes and falling back to 4.5 mmol/L by 180 minutes after ugali (300 g), sukuma wiki (100 g) and beans (100 g). Learners predict whether the protein in beans and the oil in the meal could also cause the spike. They then carry out the biuret test on beans, milk, nyama choma and ugali, and the grease-spot and emulsion tests on groundnuts, mandazi, milk and ugali. In Explain they compare the structure and roles of proteins and lipids (amino acids; fatty acids and glycerol) with carbohydrates, and use the meal data (about 20 g protein, 10 g fat, 70 g carbohydrate in ugali) to argue that carbohydrates are the main cause of the spike. The DQB and the class flow-diagram model are then revised.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners look again at Wanjiru's glucose graph (0 min: 4.5; 15 min: 5.5; 30 min: 7.0; 60 min: 8.0 peak; 90 min: 6.5; 120 min: 5.2; 150 min: 4.7; 180 min: 4.5 mmol/L) and the meal list. Individually they write a prediction: 'Beans contain about 20 g protein and the meal has about 10 g fat from cooking oil. Could these also cause the glucose spike? Which of milk, nyama choma, groundnuts, mandazi and ugali will contain protein, and which lipid?' They sketch a prediction table (food: protein? lipid?) with a reason, then share with a partner (3 minutes) before the class vote.",
        "teacherMoves": "Display the graph and say: \"Last lesson we linked the 70 g of carbohydrate in ugali to this spike. But Wanjiru's plate also had beans and oil. Do those cause the spike too? Write your prediction and one reason.\" Give 2 minutes silent writing. WAIT TIME: 10-15 seconds after asking, \"Which foods do you think contain protein?\" before taking answers. Cold-call 4 learners from different groups, asking \"What made you think that?\" Record predictions on the board in two columns (protein / lipid) without correcting. Ask: \"Which test method from Lesson 3 could we adapt to check?\" and take 2 responses.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Prediction with reasoning (commit-then-test): writing a prediction and a reason before any evidence exposes prior ideas, such as 'all food becomes sugar', and gives learners a stake in the results, so the later data confirm or challenge their thinking.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) each learner has a written prediction with a reason; (2) at least 3 learners link a test to a food chemical (for example 'iodine is for starch so we need a different test'); (3) the teacher records common misconceptions, such as 'beans are sugary so they cause the spike' or 'oil turns to glucose immediately', for revisiting in Explain."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "In groups of 4 learners carry out two tests. Test A, biuret test for protein: put about 2 cm3 of each food sample or extract (bean paste mixed with water, milk, minced nyama choma, ugali paste) in separate test tubes, add 2 cm3 of dilute sodium hydroxide, then add 2-3 drops of dilute copper sulphate and shake. Test B, lipids: (i) grease-spot test: rub or crush groundnuts, mandazi, a drop of milk and ugali on separate pieces of brown or filter paper, dry gently, and hold up to the light; (ii) emulsion test: shake the sample with 2 cm3 of ethanol, then pour the clear liquid into 2 cm3 of cold water and look for a cloudy white layer. They record results in a table. Expected results: biuret: beans violet/purple, milk violet, nyama choma violet, ugali pale blue/faint (negative or weak). Grease spot: groundnuts translucent spot and cloudy white emulsion (positive), mandazi translucent spot (positive, cooked in oil), milk faint translucent spot or weak emulsion, ugali negative. Learners add a 'carbohydrate' column using Lesson 3 results (ugali blue-black with iodine) to compare.",
        "teacherMoves": "Before starting, model each test for 2 minutes and say: \"Goggles on, gloves on, and nobody tastes anything. Why do we use a fresh tube for each food?\" Cold-call 2 groups to answer. Circulate every 5 minutes and ask: \"What colour do you see? What does that tell you about this food?\" Prompt: \"Compare your violet with the blue control; is it really different?\" Ask for water as a control (blue for biuret, no spot for the paper). WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after asking, \"What does a translucent spot mean?\" before accepting answers. Ensure learners with allergies are only recording. Ask each group to put results on a class chart; cold-call 3 groups to report one result each.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Structured data collection with a class results table and controls: pooling results across groups shows patterns (beans, milk and nyama choma are positive for protein; groundnuts and mandazi are positive for lipids; ugali is negative for both) and teaches learners that tests need controls to be trusted.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) correct safe handling (goggles, gloves, small volumes, no tasting) in at least 90% of groups; (2) results correctly recorded using the colour conventions (violet/purple = protein; translucent spot or cloudy white layer = lipid); (3) learners include a control and notice ugali is negative for protein and lipids but positive for starch; (4) the teacher checks that each group's table has all 4 foods for each test."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners use their results to build a comparison table of carbohydrates, proteins and lipids: test used, positive result, building blocks (glucose units; amino acids; fatty acids and glycerol), example in Wanjiru's meal and main roles (carbohydrates: main energy source; proteins: growth and repair of body tissues, and also enzymes; lipids: energy storage and insulation). They then use the numbers (about 70 g carbohydrate in ugali plus 15 g in beans; about 20 g protein; about 10 g fat) and the graph to write an evidence-based claim: 'Carbohydrates, not proteins or lipids, are the main cause of the spike from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L because...' They also answer: 'Why do beans, milk and groundnuts still matter for Wanjiru even though they are not the main cause of the spike?' using growth, repair and energy storage.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: \"Look at your tables. What evidence tells us the spike comes from ugali rather than from the oil?\" WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, then cold-call 3 learners. Draw simple structure diagrams on the board: a chain of glucose units, a chain of amino acids and a glycerol with three fatty acids, and ask: \"How is a protein built differently from starch?\" (cold-call 2). Ask: \"Wanjiru's meal had about 70 g carbohydrate and about 10 g fat. Which would you expect to raise blood glucose more? Why?\" Use the Claim-Evidence-Reasoning frame on the board. Address any misconception noted in Predict, such as 'beans cause the spike because they are sweet', by pointing to the evidence. Pair-share for 3 minutes, then have 2 pairs present claims; the class gives one question or critique per presentation. Clarify that proteins and lipids are digested too, but not mainly into glucose; the full processes come in Lessons 5 and 6, so do not teach them now.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning with comparison: learners compare structures, tests and roles across the three classes and then justify a claim with their own data and the meal numbers, so that they link chemical differences to the phenomenon.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) comparison table is complete and accurate (biuret/violet/amino acids; grease spot and emulsion/fatty acids and glycerol); (2) the claim cites at least 2 pieces of evidence (for example ugali negative for protein and lipid but positive for starch; 70 g carbohydrate versus 20 g protein and 10 g fat); (3) learners distinguish roles (growth and repair versus energy); (4) exit prompt: 'Name one test for protein and one for lipid and say what a positive result looks like', with at least 80% correct."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revisit the class DQB. They place green sticky notes next to questions now answered, for example 'What chemicals are in food?' (carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, with tests), and write new questions on yellow notes, such as 'What happens to proteins in the body?', 'Are proteins digested too?', 'Do enzymes work on proteins and lipids?' and 'How does glucose leave the blood?' They sort questions into: answered, to be answered in Lesson 5 (enzymes, water and minerals), and to be answered in Lesson 6 (absorption, respiration, insulin).",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: \"Look at the board. Which questions can we answer with evidence from today's tests?\" Allow WAIT TIME of 10-15 seconds, then cold-call 3 learners to move or mark a note and justify it with evidence. Ask: \"What new question has our protein and lipid evidence created?\" and take at least 4 new questions. Reply to unrelated questions with: \"Great question; where could we find out?\" Point out that enzymes are proteins, which sets up Lesson 5.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Driving Question Board as a living record: learners see their questions answered with evidence and generate new ones, which keeps the investigation learner-driven and builds ownership of the storyline.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) at least 3 questions are moved to 'answered' with a stated piece of evidence; (2) every group adds at least 1 new, testable question; (3) at least 2 new questions refer to what happens to proteins or lipids after eating, showing connection to the phenomenon."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revise the class flow-diagram model: Food chemicals, then digestion by enzymes, then glucose in blood, then cells (respiration) and storage, then back to normal. In groups they expand the 'Food chemicals' box to show carbohydrates (starch in ugali, about 70 g), proteins (beans, about 20 g) and lipids (cooking oil, groundnuts, mandazi, about 10 g), with the test for each and its building blocks. They draw a thick arrow from carbohydrates to 'glucose in blood' (main cause of the spike) and thin dotted arrows from proteins and lipids to 'growth, repair and energy storage'. They label question marks where they do not yet know the mechanism (enzymes, absorption, insulin). Finally they complete the summary table prompt.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: \"Update our model with today's evidence. Where do proteins and lipids belong, and why is the arrow thinner?\" Give 8 minutes in groups and use WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before asking for explanations. Cold-call 3 groups to explain one change and the evidence for it. Ask: \"What is still missing from the model that explains how the spike falls from 8.0 at 60 minutes to 4.5 mmol/L at 180 minutes?\" Encourage question marks for missing mechanisms. Close with: \"You didn't just learn ABOUT science—you DID science!\" and ask learners to note how they feel about the process (accomplished, confident).",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision: adding new evidence to a shared flow diagram helps learners see how each lesson contributes to explaining the glucose graph and marks gaps for later lessons.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: (1) the revised model correctly shows carbohydrates as the main source of glucose and proteins and lipids as separate, thinner pathways; (2) each group cites at least 1 test result as evidence for a change; (3) groups identify at least 1 gap (for example how starch is broken down); (4) learners show confidence by explaining their model unprompted in final share."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "Which learners confused protein and lipid test results, and did the control tubes and the comparison with carbohydrates help? Did learners use numbers (about 70 g carbohydrate, 20 g protein, 10 g fat) and the graph to argue that carbohydrates cause the spike, or did they rely on assumptions like 'beans are sweet'? Were safety routines followed, and were learners with allergies fully included? Which DQB questions about enzymes, absorption and insulin should be prioritised for Lessons 5 and 6?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that beans, milk and nyama choma turned violet/purple with the biuret test (protein), while ugali stayed pale blue. Groundnuts and mandazi left a translucent grease spot, groundnuts gave a cloudy white emulsion, and milk gave a faint result, while ugali was negative for lipids.",
      "learned": "We learned that proteins are made of amino acids and lipids are made of fatty acids and glycerol, whereas carbohydrates are made of glucose units. Proteins are used for growth and repair, lipids for energy storage, and carbohydrates are the main energy source.",
      "explained": "We explain that Wanjiru's blood glucose spike from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L at 60 minutes is mainly caused by carbohydrates (about 70 g in ugali), not by the about 20 g protein or about 10 g fat in her meal. Proteins and lipids are still used by the body, but not mainly to raise blood glucose."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 5,
    "title": "Water, Mineral Salts and Enzymes in Digestion",
    "duration": "80 minutes (double lesson)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 1.2: Chemicals of Life",
    "aresKeywords": "enzyme, amylase, catalyst, digestion, starch, maltose, reducing sugar, denatured, control, water, mineral salts, 37 degrees C",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "To investigate how saliva (salivary amylase) digests starch to reducing sugar, and how water, mineral salts and temperature affect enzyme action, so that learners can explain how the starch in Wanjiru's ugali becomes the glucose that causes her blood glucose to rise from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L.",
      "knowledge": "Enzymes are biological catalysts that are proteins. Salivary amylase digests starch to maltose, which is then broken down to glucose. Digestion is the breakdown of large insoluble food molecules into small soluble ones. Water and mineral salts are inorganic compounds that support enzyme action: water is the medium in which reactions happen, and mineral salts help maintain the conditions cells need. Enzymes are denatured by boiling and inactive at 0 degrees C, and work best at about 37 degrees C in the body.",
      "skills": "Planning and carrying out an investigation with a control (SEP 3); using iodine and Benedict's tests to collect evidence; recording and interpreting colour changes over time (SEP 4); constructing explanations from evidence (SEP 6); revising a model (SEP 2); asking questions (SEP 1).",
      "attitudes": "Curiosity about what happens to food inside the body; respect for evidence; teamwork and responsibility when handling hot water and chemicals; appreciation that everyday food processing in our bodies is chemistry.",
      "keyInquiry": "How do the chemicals of life contribute to the functioning of living organisms? (linked to: What are the chemicals of life?)",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lessons 3 and 4 showed that ugali is rich in starch (about 70 g carbohydrate) and that proteins differ from carbohydrates in structure and role. This lesson answers the question: how does starch become glucose that can enter the blood? Learners gather the evidence for the digestion step in the class model (food chemicals, then digestion by enzymes, then glucose in blood). Lesson 6 will use this to explain absorption, respiration, storage and insulin, and the fall back to 4.5 mmol/L.",
      "safetyNotes": "Each learner uses only their own clean, labelled cup and collects their own saliva (never share saliva; wash hands afterwards; use a clean cup per learner or have the teacher or a volunteer supply a pooled sample from consenting learners, discarded after use). Wear safety goggles or eye protection when heating Benedict's solution. Heat test tubes in a hot water bath, not in direct flame; point the tube mouth away from people; use a test tube holder. Hot water baths must be at about 37 degrees C for the incubation and boiling only under the teacher's supervision. Iodine stains skin and clothes; wipe spills immediately. Do not taste any chemicals or test materials. Dispose of mixtures as the teacher instructs and wash hands at the end."
    },
    "overview": "Mr Otieno reminds the class of Wanjiru's blood glucose graph: 4.5 mmol/L before the meal, a peak of 8.0 mmol/L at 60 minutes after the plate of ugali, sukuma wiki and beans, then back to 4.5 mmol/L by 180 minutes. Learners already know ugali is mostly starch (Lesson 3) and that starch is too large to enter the blood directly. Learners predict what happens to starch when it meets saliva, then run the saliva and starch investigation: 2 cm3 of 1% starch solution plus 1 cm3 of saliva at about 37 degrees C, tested with iodine at 0, 5 and 10 minutes and with Benedict's at 10 minutes, alongside a control with boiled saliva or water. They explain the results using the idea of enzymes as protein catalysts, consider the roles of water and mineral salts and the effect of temperature, then add questions to the DQB and revise the class flow-diagram model to include digestion by enzymes.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(10 minutes) Learners look again at Wanjiru's blood glucose graph (0 min: 4.5; 15 min: 5.5; 30 min: 7.0; 60 min: 8.0 peak; 90 min: 6.5; 120 min: 5.2; 150 min: 4.7; 180 min: 4.5 mmol/L). They recall that ugali has about 70 g carbohydrate, mostly starch, and that starch gives a blue-black colour with iodine and no colour change with Benedict's before digestion. Individually they write and draw a prediction in their notebooks: 'What do you think happens to starch when it is mixed with saliva in a warm tube? What colour will the iodine test give after 0, 5 and 10 minutes? What would happen if the saliva was boiled first? Why?' They also predict whether Benedict's test will change at 10 minutes. They share in pairs and then add their ideas to a class prediction chart.",
        "teacherMoves": "Display the graph. Say: 'Wanjiru's glucose went from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L in 60 minutes. But ugali is starch, not glucose. Starch is a big molecule. So what must happen to starch before it can raise glucose in the blood?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds in silence before taking answers. Ask: 'Have you ever noticed that if you chew ugali or bread for a long time it starts to taste slightly sweet? What might be happening in your mouth?' Cold-call 3 learners (not volunteers) to share their predictions, and ask each: 'What makes you think so?' Record without correcting. Ask: 'What do you think boiling the saliva will do?' Cold-call 2 more learners. Write all predictions on the board under headings 'Starch + saliva' and 'Starch + boiled saliva'. Say: 'Keep your prediction; today we will test it with evidence.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Prediction with reasoning (Predict-Observe-Explain). Writing a prediction and a reason before seeing results makes learners' existing ideas about digestion visible, creates a stake in the outcome, and lets them later compare evidence with their own thinking, which deepens conceptual change. Linking prediction to the graph connects the investigation to the anchoring phenomenon.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check notebooks as you circulate for: (1) a prediction that mentions the iodine colour changing over time; (2) a reason that links starch breakdown to sugar or glucose; (3) any mention of what saliva contains or does; (4) any prediction about the boiled saliva. Tally on a clipboard how many learners predict that the starch will disappear, that sugar will appear, and that boiled saliva will not work. Note misconceptions such as 'saliva just makes food wet' or 'the starch turns directly into blood'. Use the tally to decide how much to emphasise the control."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(30 minutes) In groups of 4, learners carry out the saliva and starch investigation. Step 1: Label tubes A (starch + saliva) and B (control: starch + boiled saliva or water). Step 2: Place 2 cm3 of 1% starch solution in each tube. Step 3: Put a drop of iodine in each well of a white tile or spotting plate and take the 0 min sample from each tube; record the colour (both blue-black). Step 4: Add 1 cm3 of saliva to tube A and 1 cm3 of boiled saliva (or water) to tube B; place both in a water bath at about 37 degrees C. Step 5: At 5 min and 10 min, use a clean dropper to take a drop from each tube into fresh iodine drops; record the colours (tube A: brown-black at 5 min, yellow-brown at 10 min; tube B: blue-black throughout). Step 6: At 10 min, add Benedict's solution to a sample of each tube and heat in a hot water bath for a few minutes; record the colours (tube A: brick-red; tube B: blue or no change). They complete a results table with columns for time, iodine colour (tube A and tube B), and Benedict's colour at 10 min. They also observe a teacher demonstration or discuss data on temperature: saliva and starch at 0 degrees C (in ice) stays blue-black, which shows the enzyme is inactive at 0 degrees C, and boiled saliva has no effect because the enzyme is denatured.",
        "teacherMoves": "Before starting say: 'You are scientists today. Each group has a control tube. Why do you think we need tube B?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds, then cold-call 2 groups. Review safety: 'Own cup only, goggles on when heating, tube mouths pointing away from people.' Circulate and ask each group: 'What colour is tube A now compared with tube B? What does that tell you about the starch?' Prompt precise language: 'Say starch present or starch absent, not just a colour.' At the 10 min mark ask: 'What colour is tube A with iodine? What does that mean? Then why did Benedict's turn brick-red?' If groups differ from the expected colours, ask them to check timing, temperature and volume rather than telling them the answer. Ask groups to take a photo or sketch the final colours. Prompt reading of the table: 'Look at tube A from 0 to 10 min. What pattern do you see in the iodine colour?' Cold-call 3 learners to describe the pattern using evidence only. Introduce the ice-bath result or class data: 'At 0 degrees C the starch stayed blue-black. What does this tell you about the enzyme at low temperature?' Ensure all waste is collected and hands washed.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Controlled investigation with data table (Planning and carrying out investigations; analysing data). The control tube isolates the variable (active saliva versus boiled saliva), so learners can attribute the colour changes to something in saliva rather than to warmth or time. Recording iodine at three time points shows change over time, linking evidence to the idea of progressive breakdown.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Look for: (1) learners labelling tubes correctly and using the same volumes (2 cm3 starch, 1 cm3 saliva) in both tubes; (2) accurate colour recording at each time point; (3) use of the control to compare; (4) correct safety behaviour (goggles, holder, tube angle); (5) learners stating that iodine changing from blue-black to yellow-brown means starch has gone and the Benedict's brick-red means reducing sugar is present. Use a checklist with a tick for each group. Ask each group to show their results table before leaving the practical."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(20 minutes) Learners return to their predictions and compare them with the evidence. In groups they write an explanation (claim, evidence, reasoning) for: 'How did saliva change the starch?' Claim: Saliva digested starch into reducing sugar. Evidence: iodine changed from blue-black (0 min) to brown-black (5 min) to yellow-brown (10 min), the Benedict's test at 10 min was brick-red, and the control with boiled saliva stayed blue-black. Reasoning: Saliva contains salivary amylase, an enzyme, which is a protein that acts as a biological catalyst, speeding up the breakdown of starch to maltose, which is then broken to glucose. Boiling denatured the enzyme so the starch stayed unchanged. Then learners discuss: (a) Why is the water bath at about 37 degrees C? (the temperature at which enzymes work best in the body); (b) Why did the enzyme not work at 0 degrees C or after boiling? (inactive at low temperature; denatured at high temperature); (c) What roles do water and mineral salts play? (Both are inorganic, as learned in Lesson 2; water is the medium in which the starch solution and enzyme react and which carries the digested sugars, and mineral salts help keep the right conditions for enzymes and cells.) They link this to digestion: large insoluble starch becomes small soluble sugar. Finally they link to the phenomenon: starch in Wanjiru's ugali (about 70 g carbohydrate) is digested to glucose, which is then absorbed into the blood, contributing to the rise from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L at 60 minutes.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Look at your prediction. Was it supported by the evidence? Put a tick or a cross and explain.' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds. Model the claim-evidence-reasoning frame on the board. Ask: 'What is your claim? Which evidence supports it? What science idea links the evidence to your claim?' Cold-call 4 groups to share one part each (claim, evidence, reasoning, then a link to the graph). Probe: 'Lesson 4 told us enzymes are proteins. What happened to the protein in the boiled saliva? Why did the starch stay blue-black?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds. Ask: 'Is the enzyme used up in the reaction? What does catalyst mean?' Clarify: a catalyst speeds up a reaction and is not used up. Ask: 'Water and mineral salts are inorganic. What role might they play in digestion?' Cold-call 2 learners, then give the key explanation. Use the term 'digestion' (breakdown of large insoluble food molecules into small soluble ones) and avoid using 'absorption' details, which are for Lesson 6. Ask: 'Why do you think the body works best at about 37 degrees C? What would happen to the enzymes in a person with a very high fever?' Close with: 'So how does this investigation help explain the rise in Wanjiru's blood glucose?' Cold-call 3 learners, correcting misconceptions such as 'saliva turns starch into glucose instantly'.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER) with a causal explanation. CER requires learners to connect observed data (colour changes, control) to a scientific principle (enzymes as protein catalysts), which builds a mechanistic explanation. Returning to the predictions promotes reflection on changed thinking, and linking back to the graph connects the lab to the phenomenon.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check written explanations for: (1) a claim that starch was digested to sugar; (2) at least two pieces of evidence (iodine change and Benedict's result, plus the control); (3) reasoning that names the enzyme amylase and states that it is a protein and a catalyst; (4) a correct explanation for the boiled saliva (denatured) and for 0 degrees C (inactive); (5) mention of water or mineral salts as inorganic supporting chemicals; (6) a link to the rise in Wanjiru's blood glucose. Use a 3-point rubric (claim, evidence, reasoning) and note groups needing support. Use a quick exit question: 'What does the control tell us?'"
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "(10 minutes) Learners revisit the class DQB with the driving question: 'What chemicals are in the food we eat, and what happens to them in our bodies?' In groups they use sticky notes to (1) mark questions that today's evidence helps to answer (for example: 'How do cells break down chemicals?' partly answered: starch is digested by enzymes to sugars; 'What chemicals are in food?' extended: water and mineral salts support digestion) and (2) write new questions that arise, such as: 'How does the digested glucose get from the gut into the blood?', 'What does the body do with glucose once it is in the blood?', 'Why does the glucose level fall back to 4.5 mmol/L?', 'Do enzymes digest proteins and fats too?', 'What happens to enzymes in a person with a high fever?' They place the notes on the DQB and move answered notes to a 'Partly answered' column with a short evidence note.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Look at the board. Which questions can we answer, even partly, with what we found today?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds. Invite 3 learners (cold-call) to move a note and say what evidence supports the move. Then ask: 'What new questions do you have after seeing starch turn to sugar?' Give groups 3 minutes to write at least 2 new questions each. Cold-call 3 groups to read one new question aloud and explain why it matters for Wanjiru's graph. Highlight questions that point to Lesson 6: absorption, use of glucose in cells, storage and insulin. Say: 'These are exactly the questions we will tackle next lesson.' Make sure the question 'Why does glucose not stay high forever?' is visible and starred.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Driving Question Board as a living record of learning. Sorting questions into answered, partly answered and new makes the growth in understanding visible and gives learners ownership of the inquiry, while new questions create the need to know for Lesson 6.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observe whether learners (1) correctly identify which questions the saliva investigation addresses, (2) cite specific evidence (iodine colours, Benedict's result, control) when moving notes, (3) write new questions that are testable or point to the next missing step (absorption, respiration, insulin), and (4) link questions back to the graph. Count the number of groups that generate at least one question about what happens to glucose after digestion."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "(10 minutes) Learners revise the class flow-diagram model: Food chemicals, then digestion by enzymes, then glucose in blood, then cells (respiration) and storage, then back to normal. In groups they add to the first two boxes: under 'Food chemicals' they write starch (from ugali, maize flour), protein (beans), lipids (cooking oil), water and mineral salts; under 'Digestion by enzymes' they write: amylase (a protein catalyst in saliva) digests starch to maltose, which is broken to glucose; water is the medium; works best at about 37 degrees C; denatured by boiling; inactive at 0 degrees C. They label evidence next to the arrow: 'iodine blue-black to yellow-brown; Benedict's brick-red at 10 min; boiled saliva control stays blue-black'. They add a question mark to the arrow between 'digestion' and 'glucose in blood' with the note 'absorption: Lesson 6'. Each group then completes the summary table prompt (what we observed, what we learned, how we explain it) and one group presents. The lesson ends with the celebration: learners check that they feel accomplished, value the process and are confident.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Our model has a gap. Where in the model did today's evidence fit?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds. Ask groups to revise their diagram for 5 minutes. Circulate and ask: 'What evidence supports this box? What would you say to Wanjiru if she asked why her glucose rose?' Cold-call 3 groups to explain their revised model in 2 sentences using the words enzyme, catalyst, starch and glucose. Correct any use of 'simple sugar' (use 'reducing sugar' for the Benedict's test) and check that learners do not yet claim details about insulin or glycogen, which belong to Lesson 6. Update the large class model on the wall with agreed changes. Close with: 'You didn't just learn ABOUT science, you DID science!' Ask learners to tick: I feel accomplished, I value the process, I am confident. Ensure equipment is cleaned and hands washed.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision (Developing and using models). Adding new evidence and mechanisms to the same flow diagram each lesson shows how scientific ideas build, and requires learners to connect parts of the system (food chemicals, enzyme digestion, glucose) to the single phenomenon. Marking gaps with question marks shows models are works in progress.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Check revised models for: (1) starch correctly placed as the food chemical digested; (2) amylase named as an enzyme, a protein and a catalyst; (3) temperature conditions (about 37 degrees C best, denatured by boiling, inactive at 0 degrees C); (4) evidence labelled on the arrow; (5) a gap identified between digestion and glucose in blood. Listen to group explanations for correct vocabulary (digestion, enzyme, catalyst, denatured, reducing sugar). Use the summary table responses as an exit check and record learners who still say that saliva is 'used up' or that starch becomes glucose without an enzyme."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson consider: (1) Did every group use the control tube to justify their claim about enzyme action, or did some just report colours? (2) Which learners still think that boiling or cold only 'slows' the enzyme rather than denaturing or inactivating it? (3) Was the 37 degrees C water bath stable enough for reliable results, and how could timing be improved? (4) Did learners link starch digestion back to the rise in Wanjiru's glucose from 4.5 to 8.0 mmol/L, or did they treat the investigation as separate? (5) Which new DQB questions will guide Lesson 6 (absorption, respiration, storage and insulin), and are all learners ready to explain why glucose falls back to 4.5 mmol/L? (6) Did the safety routines for saliva, hot water and iodine work well for all groups?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that with saliva at about 37 degrees C the iodine test changed from blue-black at 0 min, to brown-black at 5 min, to yellow-brown at 10 min, and Benedict's test at 10 min was brick-red, while the control with boiled saliva or water stayed blue-black.",
      "learned": "We learned that saliva contains amylase, an enzyme that is a protein and a biological catalyst, which digests starch to maltose and then glucose; water and mineral salts are inorganic chemicals that support this, and enzymes work best at about 37 degrees C, are denatured by boiling and inactive at 0 degrees C.",
      "explained": "We can explain that the starch in Wanjiru's ugali is digested by enzymes such as amylase into glucose, which helps explain why her blood glucose concentration rises from 4.5 to a peak of 8.0 mmol/L at 60 minutes; we still need to find out how glucose gets into the blood and why it falls back to 4.5 mmol/L by 180 minutes."
    }
  },
  {
    "number": 6,
    "title": "Absorption, Respiration and Regulation: Explaining the Fall Back to 4.5 mmol/L",
    "duration": "80 minutes (double lesson)",
    "substrand": "Sub-Strand 1.2: Chemicals of Life",
    "aresKeywords": "absorption, blood glucose concentration, insulin, pancreas, respiration, ATP, glycogen, liver, muscles, regulation, final explanation, model",
    "slo": {
      "purpose": "To help learners complete the explanation of Wanjiru's blood glucose graph by showing what happens to glucose after digestion: it is absorbed into the blood, taken up by cells under the influence of insulin, used in respiration to release energy and stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles. Learners then finalise and present the class model that answers the driving question.",
      "knowledge": "Learners explain that (1) digestion of starch in ugali by enzymes such as amylase produces glucose, which is absorbed into the blood, causing the rise from 4.5 to the peak of 8.0 mmol/L at 60 min; (2) the pancreas releases insulin, which causes cells, the liver and muscles to take up glucose; (3) cells use glucose in respiration (glucose + oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + energy, ATP released); (4) the liver and muscles store excess glucose as glycogen; (5) the level falls by 3.5 mmol/L over 120 min (60 to 180 min) back to 4.5 mmol/L; (6) proteins and lipids are also digested and used for growth, repair and energy storage, but carbohydrates are the main cause of the spike.",
      "skills": "Constructing explanations from evidence (NGSS SEP 6); developing and using models (SEP 2); analysing and interpreting data (SEP 4): calculating rates of rise and fall from the glucose table; engaging in argument from evidence (SEP 7); obtaining, evaluating and communicating information (SEP 8) through the final model presentation.",
      "attitudes": "Learners show curiosity and persistence in solving the puzzle, respect for peers' ideas during presentations and peer critique, appreciation of how the body regulates its chemicals, and confidence in using evidence. Learners value the process of science: 'You didn't just learn ABOUT science—you DID science!'",
      "keyInquiry": "1. What are the chemicals of life? 2. How do the chemicals of life contribute to the functioning of living organisms? Driving question: What chemicals are in the food we eat, and what happens to them in our bodies?",
      "purposeInStoryline": "Lessons 1 to 5 gave the graph, the chemicals in the meal, the carbohydrate tests linking ugali starch to glucose, the roles of proteins and lipids, and the enzyme digestion of starch to sugars. Lesson 6 answers the last open question on the DQB: why the glucose does not stay high. It closes the storyline with the Final Explanation, a comparison of the Lesson 1 model with the final model, and DQB completion.",
      "safetyNotes": "This lesson uses no chemicals or heating. Learners use only the data table, role-play cards and chart paper. Keep walkways clear during the role-play. Handle food tasting only if clean, allergy checks are done and the teacher allows it (no tasting is required). Be sensitive to learners who live with diabetes or have family members who do; use the topic respectfully and do not ask learners to share health information. Ensure learners wash hands before any snack."
    },
    "overview": "Learners return to Wanjiru's graph from Nairobi (4.5 mmol/L at 0 min, peak 8.0 at 60 min, back to 4.5 at 180 min) and finish explaining it. After predicting what happens to glucose once it is in the blood, they analyse the falling part of the graph (8.0, 6.5, 5.2, 4.7, 4.5), act out a role-play of glucose absorption, insulin release from the pancreas, uptake by cells, respiration and glycogen storage in the liver and muscles. Learners then write a Final Explanation, complete the DQB, compare the Lesson 1 model with the final flow-diagram model (Food chemicals, then digestion by enzymes, then glucose in blood, then cells (respiration) and storage, then back to normal) and present it in a gallery walk and celebration.",
    "framework": [
      {
        "phase": "Predict Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners look again at Wanjiru's graph (Mr Otieno's class, Nairobi) with the table 0 min: 4.5; 15: 5.5; 30: 7.0; 60: 8.0 (peak); 90: 6.5; 120: 5.2; 150: 4.7; 180: 4.5 mmol/L. Individually (2 min) they draw on a mini whiteboard or in their notebook: 'Where does the glucose go after 60 minutes?' with a sketch of Wanjiru's body and arrows. They write one prediction: 'The glucose goes down because ______.' They also predict: 'What would the graph look like if nothing removed glucose from the blood?' and sketch a dotted line on a copy of the graph. Then they pair-share with a neighbour and note one similarity and one difference.",
        "teacherMoves": "Display the graph and say: 'Remember Wanjiru's lunch of ugali, sukuma wiki and beans. By 60 minutes, her blood glucose is 8.0 mmol/L. Today we finish the puzzle: where does the glucose go?' Point to the falling part of the graph. Say: 'Draw your idea first, no talking.' Give 2 minutes. Then ask: 'What would happen to the graph if nothing removed glucose from the blood?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds before taking responses. Cold-call 4 learners to share predictions without judging, e.g. 'Akinyi, what did you draw? Why?' Record all ideas on a chart headed 'Our predictions' (e.g. 'used for energy', 'passes out in urine', 'stored', 'something controls it'). Say: 'Keep these ideas; we will test them against evidence.' Circulate and note misconceptions such as 'glucose is lost in sweat or urine'.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Predict-Observe-Explain with an initial model sketch. Making predictions visible before new evidence activates prior knowledge from Lessons 1 to 5 (the graph, glucose from starch digestion) and exposes misconceptions, so learners are motivated to look for evidence that supports or changes their ideas.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Look for: (a) at least 8 of 10 learners can sketch a falling line or arrows for glucose leaving the blood; (b) learners use ideas from earlier lessons (starch digested to glucose, enzymes); (c) identify misconceptions (glucose is just lost in urine or sweat; glucose is destroyed; glucose simply 'disappears'). Tally on a clipboard how many learners mention 'energy', 'storage' or 'a hormone'. Use this to decide how much time to spend in the Observe phase."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Observe Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Part 1 (15 min): Data analysis in groups of 4. Using the glucose table, learners calculate: the rise from 4.5 to 8.0 = 3.5 mmol/L in 60 min; the fall from 8.0 to 4.5 = 3.5 mmol/L over 120 min (60 to 180 min); and the fall in each interval (60 to 90: 1.5; 90 to 120: 1.3; 120 to 150: 0.5; 150 to 180: 0.2 mmol/L). They compare: 'The rise is faster (3.5 over 60 min) than the fall (3.5 over 120 min).' They note the level is back in the normal fasting range of 4.0 to 5.5 mmol/L by 150 min (4.7). Part 2 (20 min): Role-play 'Journey of the glucose'. Groups of 8 each get role cards: glucose molecules (from ugali starch), blood (a rope loop), small intestine wall (absorption), pancreas (holds insulin cards), body cells (with an 'oxygen' card), liver and muscle (with 'glycogen' storage boxes). Steps: (1) amylase digestion has produced glucose (cards from Lesson 5); (2) glucose is absorbed into the blood (students step into the rope loop, blood glucose rises); (3) the pancreas releases insulin when glucose is high; (4) insulin causes cells, liver and muscles to take up glucose; (5) in cells, glucose + oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + energy (ATP released); (6) the liver and muscles store excess glucose as glycogen; (7) blood glucose returns to 4.5 mmol/L. One observer per group records on a checklist how many glucose cards are left in the blood loop at each step and matches it to the graph (peak at 60, 6.5 at 90, 5.2 at 120, 4.7 at 150, 4.5 at 180).",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Scientists look for patterns in data. Calculate the rise and the fall. Which is faster?' Move around and ask: 'How did you get 3.5 for the fall? Show me the two values.' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after each question. For the role-play, hand out role cards and state: 'Observers, your job is to count the glucose cards left in the blood loop after each step.' Pause the action at step 4 and ask: 'Why is the pancreas holding up insulin cards now and not at 0 minutes?' (Blood glucose is high at the peak, 8.0 mmol/L.) Cold-call 3 observers: 'How many glucose cards are left in the blood now? Which time on the graph is this?' Ask: 'Where does the energy in respiration come from? What gas do your cells need?' Link to breathing: 'That is why you breathe harder after running to catch a matatu.' Reinforce vocabulary: digestion (breakdown into small soluble molecules), absorption (movement into the blood). Do not re-teach enzyme action; simply recall: 'In Lesson 5, we saw amylase break starch to maltose and then glucose.' Challenge: 'Proteins and lipids from beans and cooking oil are also digested. Do they cause the spike? Use your Lesson 4 results.' (They are used for growth, repair and energy storage; carbohydrates are the main cause of the spike.)",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Data analysis plus embodied modelling (role-play). Calculating rates turns the graph into evidence of a regulating process, and acting out the invisible journey makes abstract ideas concrete (absorption, insulin, respiration, glycogen). The observers' counts link the model to the data, building the cause-and-effect reasoning needed for a scientific explanation.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: learners correctly calculate 3.5 mmol/L for both rise and fall and state that the fall takes twice as long as the rise (120 min versus 60 min); observers' checklists show glucose cards decreasing in step with 8.0, 6.5, 5.2, 4.7, 4.5; learners can name which role (pancreas) releases insulin and which organs store glycogen (liver and muscles). Check that learners use 'absorption' and 'respiration' correctly. Probe those who say 'insulin is food' or 'glycogen is sugar in urine'."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Explain Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners write the Final Explanation individually (12 min) in the Claim-Evidence-Reasoning format to answer the driving question: 'What chemicals are in the food we eat, and what happens to them in our bodies?' using Wanjiru's meal. Claim: Why does blood glucose rise and then fall? Evidence: at least four pieces of data or test results (e.g. 70 g carbohydrate in ugali, blue-black iodine for ugali; peak 8.0 mmol/L at 60 min; saliva digestion at 37 degrees C turned starch to reducing sugar, brick-red at 10 min; fall of 3.5 mmol/L over 120 min; 4.7 at 150 and 4.5 at 180). Reasoning: explain the sequence: starch is digested by enzymes such as amylase into glucose; glucose is absorbed into the blood so concentration rises to 8.0; the pancreas releases insulin; cells, liver and muscles take up glucose; cells use it in respiration to release energy (glucose + oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + energy, ATP released); liver and muscles store the excess as glycogen; the blood level falls back to 4.5 mmol/L by 180 min. They also state the roles of proteins (beans, growth and repair) and lipids (cooking oil, energy storage) and why carbohydrates are the main cause of the spike. Then, in groups of 4, they peer-review using a checklist and revise one sentence.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'You are now the scientist. Explain the whole story from the plate of ugali to the graph returning to normal.' Show the CER frame on the board with sentence starters: 'The data show that...', 'This is because...', 'Insulin causes...'. WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after posing: 'Which piece of evidence best shows that the glucose came from the starch in ugali?' Cold-call 3 learners for evidence and 2 for reasoning. Circulate with a checklist: Does the explanation include (1) digestion by enzymes, (2) absorption, (3) insulin from the pancreas, (4) respiration with the equation, (5) glycogen in the liver and muscles, (6) numbers from the table? Prompt gaps: 'You wrote that glucose disappears. Where does it go? What chemical equation shows what cells do with it?' Ask a challenge: 'If the pancreas released no insulin, how would the graph after 60 minutes look different?' (Level would stay high; do not extend to diseases beyond what is needed, just say regulation would fail.) After peer review, ask 3 groups to read one reasoning sentence aloud and invite the class to give a thumbs up or ask for more evidence. Conclude: 'Notice that carbohydrates are the main cause of the spike, but proteins and lipids from beans, groundnuts and mandazi are also digested and used for growth, repair and energy storage.'",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Claim-Evidence-Reasoning (CER) with peer critique. Requiring a claim linked to specific data and a chain of reasoning shifts learners from recalling facts to constructing a causal explanation, integrating evidence from all five lessons. Peer review makes reasoning visible and improves it.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Use a 4-level rubric on the written explanation: Level 4 includes all six components with correct data (3.5 mmol/L rise in 60 min, 3.5 mmol/L fall over 120 min, 8.0 peak, 4.5 at 180 min); Level 3 lacks one component; Level 2 describes the graph only; Level 1 lists facts without linking. Look for correct use of 'blood glucose concentration', 'absorption', 'respiration', 'insulin' and 'glycogen'. Target: at least 70% of learners reach Level 3 or 4. Note learners who mix up digestion and absorption or say insulin is a food, and reteach in the next session."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Driving Question Board (DQB) Creation",
        "learnerExperience": "Learners revisit the class Driving Question Board started in Lesson 1, headed 'What chemicals are in the food we eat, and what happens to them in our bodies?' In groups, they take the sticky notes of questions and sort them into 'Answered', 'Partly answered' and 'New questions'. For each answered question, they attach an evidence note naming the lesson where it was answered (e.g. 'Why does glucose rise?' answered by Lessons 3 and 5; 'What chemicals are in food?' answered by Lessons 2, 3 and 4; 'Why doesn't glucose stay high forever?' answered today). Each learner writes one new question on a different-coloured sticky note (e.g. 'How do the kidneys or hormones other than insulin affect glucose?' or 'How does exercise change the graph?' or 'How would the graph differ after eating mandazi?') and places it in the 'New questions' section.",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Let us go back to our first questions from Lesson 1. Which can we now answer with evidence?' Read 3 original questions aloud and ask: 'Who can answer this using data or a test we did?' WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds. Cold-call 5 learners to move a question to 'Answered' and justify it with a lesson and evidence. Ask: 'Which questions are only partly answered? What else would we need to find out?' Highlight that real scientists end with new questions. Say: 'Write one question that Mr Otieno could not answer from our data.' Have learners place their notes and select 2 for a 'future investigation' list (e.g. effect of exercise on the graph; comparing a meal of mandazi with one of ugali). Link back to the KICD key inquiry questions: 'What are the chemicals of life? How do the chemicals of life contribute to the functioning of living organisms?' and ask learners to point to DQB notes that answer each.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Question sorting on the Driving Question Board. Revisiting the learners' own questions shows the growth of knowledge across the unit, gives ownership of the learning and models how science generates new questions from answered ones.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Observable indicators: learners correctly match at least 80% of questions to the lesson and evidence that answered them; they can explain why a question is only 'partly answered'; new questions are testable and related to the chemicals of food or glucose regulation (not random). Count how many learners can quote a specific data value (e.g. 70 g carbohydrate, 8.0 mmol/L, 37 degrees C) when moving a note."
      },
      {
        "phase": "Model Building Phase",
        "learnerExperience": "Part 1 (10 min): Learners retrieve their Lesson 1 model (their first sketch) and the class model. In groups of 4, they build the final flow-diagram model on chart paper: Food chemicals (carbohydrates, proteins, lipids, vitamins, water, mineral salts in ugali, sukuma wiki and beans), then digestion by enzymes (starch to maltose to glucose by amylase), then glucose in blood (absorption; peak 8.0 mmol/L at 60 min), then cells (respiration: glucose + oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + energy, ATP released) and storage (liver and muscles store glycogen; insulin from the pancreas causes uptake), then back to normal (4.5 mmol/L by 180 min). They label the graph points (8.0, 6.5, 5.2, 4.7, 4.5) on a small graph beside the model and add the roles of proteins (growth and repair) and lipids (energy storage). Part 2 (15 min): Gallery walk and presentation. Two groups present for 2 min each; others visit posters and leave a 'star' (strength) and a 'step' (suggestion) sticky note. Part 3 (5 min): Model comparison. Learners place their Lesson 1 model beside the final model and complete 'At first we thought... Now we know... because of evidence from...' Closing celebration: learners tick 'I feel accomplished', 'I value the process' and 'I am confident' and the teacher says, 'You didn't just learn ABOUT science—you DID science!'",
        "teacherMoves": "Say: 'Your model must answer the driving question and explain every part of Wanjiru's graph. Where does the rise come from? Where does the fall come from?' Provide chart paper, markers and a checklist: includes digestion, absorption, insulin, respiration with equation, glycogen, data labels, arrows showing the return to normal. WAIT TIME 10-15 seconds after asking: 'What is missing from your model that would explain the fall after 60 minutes?' Circulate and ask each group: 'Show me where insulin acts. What happens to the glucose after it enters the cell?' During the gallery walk, cold-call 4 learners to give one 'star' and one 'step' to a group. Ask the class: 'How is this model different from the one we drew in Lesson 1?' Cold-call 3 learners to name one idea they added (e.g. enzymes, insulin, glycogen, respiration). Ask presenters: 'What evidence in your model came from our own tests?' Connect to the careers of nutritionists and doctors in Nairobi and Mombasa hospitals, and to farmers like Mama Achieng near Kisumu whose maize and beans provide these chemicals. Close with the celebration: 'Look at how far your thinking has come, from a puzzling graph to a full explanation. You didn't just learn ABOUT science—you DID science!' Invite 3 learners to say one thing they are proud of.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Cumulative model revision and comparison (first model versus final model), with gallery walk and peer feedback. Comparing the first and final models makes learning visible and builds the ability to see a system as a whole: food chemicals, digestion, absorption, regulation and use of energy. Peer feedback develops scientific communication and argument from evidence.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Use a model checklist: (1) all five stages in the correct order (food chemicals, digestion by enzymes, glucose in blood, cells and storage, back to normal); (2) insulin from the pancreas shown as lowering blood glucose; (3) respiration equation correct; (4) glycogen stored in the liver and muscles; (5) data labels match the table (peak 8.0 at 60, 6.5 at 90, 5.2 at 120, 4.7 at 150, 4.5 at 180); (6) proteins and lipids roles stated. Success: at least 80% of groups meet 5 of 6 criteria. Listen to presentations for correct use of key terms. Learners' 'At first we thought... Now we know...' statements should name at least two new ideas supported by evidence."
      }
    ],
    "teacherReflection": "After the lesson, reflect: (1) How many learners could explain the fall in glucose using insulin, respiration and glycogen, and which parts of the CER explanations were weakest? (2) Did the role-play help learners connect the model to the graph, or did some treat it as a game without the data link? (3) Which misconceptions remained (for example, glucose lost in urine or sweat, insulin as food, confusing digestion with absorption)? (4) Did every learner contribute to the DQB and the model presentation, including quieter learners? (5) Which learners could compare their Lesson 1 model with the final model with specific evidence? (6) What would I change, such as time for the gallery walk or support for learners who struggle with the respiration equation? Record two follow-up actions, such as a short revision quiz and an extension on how exercise changes the glucose graph.",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that after Wanjiru's meal of ugali, sukuma wiki and beans, blood glucose concentration rose from 4.5 to a peak of 8.0 mmol/L at 60 min (a rise of 3.5 mmol/L) and then fell by 3.5 mmol/L over the next 120 min, to 6.5 at 90, 5.2 at 120, 4.7 at 150 and 4.5 mmol/L at 180 min. The rise is faster than the fall. In our role-play and data analysis we saw glucose enter the blood and then leave it.",
      "learned": "We learned that starch in ugali is digested by enzymes such as amylase into glucose, which is absorbed into the blood. The pancreas releases insulin, which causes cells, the liver and muscles to take up glucose. Cells use glucose in respiration (glucose + oxygen gives carbon dioxide + water + energy, ATP released) and the liver and muscles store the excess as glycogen. Proteins and lipids are also digested and used for growth, repair and energy storage.",
      "explained": "Blood glucose rises after the meal because starch and other carbohydrates in the food are digested into glucose and absorbed into the blood. It falls back to 4.5 mmol/L by 180 min because insulin from the pancreas causes glucose to be taken up by cells, used in respiration to release energy, and stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles. Carbohydrates are the main cause of the spike, while proteins and lipids from beans, milk, groundnuts and mandazi are used for growth, repair and energy storage. This answers our driving question about the chemicals in the food we eat and what happens to them in our bodies."
    }
  }
